Cases per arm needed to detect a real improvement, at 80% power.

   baseline       +1pt     +2pt     +3pt     +5pt    +10pt
       60%     37,513    9,336    4,129    1,471      356
       75%     29,036    7,157    3,135    1,094      250
       85%     19,461    4,724    2,036      686      141
       92%     10,890    2,554    1,059      326       45

Read the +3pt column, because that is the size of improvement people argue
about most. It costs thousands of cases. Two consequences follow, and both are
more useful than the despair:

  1. Pair your comparisons. Running both systems on the same cases removes the
     case-to-case variation and cuts the requirement dramatically — often by more
     than half, and more when the systems agree often.
  2. Stop shipping 3-point changes one at a time. Batch five of them, measure the
     batch, and keep the batch or revert it. A 15-point gain from 85% is visible
     at 48 cases, a set you can actually build; from a lower
     baseline the same gain needs more.


The noise ceiling: the highest score anybody could get on your set.

Your labels are not perfect. §6.8 shows how an arguable ground truth caps every
score you report, and a case whose gold answer is wrong is a case a perfect
system fails.

   label error    ceiling   what an 85% result actually means
           0%       100%    85% of what is achievable
           3%        97%    88% of what is achievable
           5%        95%    89% of what is achievable
          10%        90%    94% of what is achievable
          15%        85%   100% of what is achievable

At 10% label error a score of 85% is 94% of everything available: only five of the
fifteen points below 100% are within reach, and the other ten are wrong labels.
Measuring the ceiling is how you find out that your model is nearly finished
and your data is not.


And the other ceiling: the same system, scored twice.

  200 runs of an unchanged system at a true rate of 85%, n=120 each:

    lowest observed    75.0%
    highest observed   92.5%
    middle 95%         79.2% - 90.8%   (12 points wide)

  Nothing changed between those runs. A team watching this dashboard would
  have celebrated an 8-point gain and investigated a 10-point regression,
  and both would have been the same system on a different Tuesday.

  This is the argument for §22.14's drift threshold against a recorded baseline,
  rather than an absolute floor set just under today's score.
